; `(-> T)` is not the type of a nullary function. A nullary function is
; declared with a BARE type - `(:: hostTarget Int)` - and a one-atom
; arrow is refused: stage0 said "-> requires at least two types" and
; `parseTypeParens` refuses it here.
;
; What was missing was the refusal REACHING anyone. `parseSigDecl` read
; the type opportunistically and dropped a failed parse on the floor, so
; this declared nothing at all and the compiler said `OK`.
(:: f (-> Int))
(fn (f) 1)
